 partners' overall activity collapses while the new partners' activity appears only after the change point, and a matched non-member's turnover lacks any shared-cohort structure \u2014 so this is a genuine abrupt regime switch, not gradual background turnover or a popularity/volume artefact."}, "raw_final_response": "**Conclusion**\n\nThe group's contacts flip coherently around t \u2248 23,600.\n\n- Every group member sampled (25, 396, 489, 1036, 1163, 1366, 1763) draws its partners from the *same* old cohort {65, 163, 292, 389, 442, 880, 978, 987, 1587, 1633, 1780, 1888, 1999} before ~23.6k and from the *same* new cohort {66, 232, 245, 422, 526, 702, 850, 986, 990, 1317, 1358, 1566, 1681} after, with essentially zero overlap (steps 10\u201319, 20\u201322, 28\u201329).\n- The switch is abrupt, not gradual: the old cohort's total activity goes 110 events in [19140,23140] \u2192 1 event in [24140,29140]; the new cohort goes 0\u20131 events before \u2192 121, 148, 140 afterwards (steps 25, 26). Node 1163 is silent in [23140,25140], marking the transition gap.\n- Confounder controls: group event volume is flat across the period (~110\u2013128 per 3k bin, step 03), so this is not a volume spike; the old partners are not merely \"unpopular later\" in a global sense while the new ones were already active earlier (steps 23, 24 show near-zero cross-period degree in both directions). A comparably active non-member (1726) also turns over partners, but into idiosyncratic singleton contacts rather than a shared cohort (steps 27, 30, 31), so the group's synchronized cohort replacement is not generic background churn.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_798d04c6\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [25, 396, 489, 638, 1036, 1163, 1244, 1366, 1417, 1489, 1729, 1763],\n  \"window\": [18640, 28640],\n  \"confidence\": 0.88,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"rol